WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.655 --> 00:00:05.530
CHRISTOPHER FLANNERY:
Hi, I'm Chris Flannery,

00:00:05.530 --> 00:00:08.290
a senior fellow of the
Claremont Institute,

00:00:08.290 --> 00:00:11.200
contributing editor of the
Claremont Review of Books,

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:14.650
and host of The
American Story podcast.

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:18.380
Every generation of
Americans from the beginning

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:23.840
has had to answer for itself the
question, how should we live?

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:28.490
Our answers, generation after
generation, in war and peace,

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:31.970
in good times, in bad
times, in small things

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:36.170
and in great things, through the
whole range of human affairs,

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:40.550
are the essential threads of
the larger American story.

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:44.090
There is an infinite variety of
these smaller American stories

00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:46.550
that shed light on the
moral and political reality

00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:48.950
of American life.

00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:51.930
And we keep creating them.

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:55.800
These fundamental experiences
known to all human beings,

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:58.590
but known to us in
an American way,

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:02.100
create the mystic chords of
memory that bind us together

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:04.650
as a people and are the
necessary beginnings

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:09.120
of any human wisdom
we might hope to find.

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:11.970
These mystic chords stretch
not only from battlefields

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:17.070
and patriot graves, but from
back roads, schoolyards and bar

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:22.890
stools, city halls, blues
joints, summer afternoons,

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:27.390
old neighborhoods, ballparks,
and deserted beaches,

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:29.100
from everywhere
you find Americans

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:32.600
being and becoming American.

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:36.970
A story may be tragic,
complicated, or hilarious.

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:39.430
But if it is a true
American story,

00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:41.410
it will be impossible
to read or listen

00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:43.720
to it attentively without
awakening the better

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:46.690
angels of our nature.

00:01:46.690 --> 00:01:48.810
So here's one about the
beautiful friendship

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:51.000
of two remarkable Americans.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:53.850
I call it "Fingertip Memories."

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:56.940
"Helen Keller was 14
years old when she first

00:01:56.940 --> 00:02:01.440
met the world-famous
Mark Twain in 1894.

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:04.130
They became fast friends.

00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:07.520
He helped arrange for her to go
to college at Radcliffe, where

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:09.800
she graduated in 1904--

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:12.800
the first deaf and blind
person in the world

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:15.500
to earn a Bachelor
of Arts degree.

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:18.540
She learned to read
English, French, German,

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:23.040
and Latin in Braille and went on
to become practically as world

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:26.520
famous as her dear friend,
writing prolifically

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:30.310
and lecturing across the
country and around the world.

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:32.590
Twain, with his
usual understatement,

00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:35.830
called her one of the two
most remarkable people

00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:38.480
in the 19th century.

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:40.510
His other candidate
was Napoleon."

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:45.680
I guess he forgot Lincoln.

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:48.920
"Keller lived into
the 1960s and shared

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:50.570
some of her fond
memories of Twain

00:02:50.570 --> 00:02:54.890
in an autobiographical
book she published in 1929.

00:02:54.890 --> 00:02:56.960
In particular, she
records recollections

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:00.080
from her last visit to her
friend in his Stormfield

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:02.800
home in Redding, Connecticut--

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:07.650
what she thought of as, in her
words, 'a land of enchantment.'

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:11.220
She preserves for us a vivid
image not only of Mark Twain--

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:13.650
Mr. Clemens, as she called him--

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:16.630
but of her own vivacious mind.

00:03:16.630 --> 00:03:19.410
About Twain, she
writes, 'There are

00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:21.690
writers who belong to the
history of their nation's

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:22.765
literature.

00:03:22.765 --> 00:03:25.630
Mark Twain is one of them.

00:03:25.630 --> 00:03:29.070
When we think of great
Americans, we think of him.

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:31.680
He incorporated the
age he lived in.

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:34.740
To me, he symbolizes
the pioneer qualities--

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:39.360
the large, free, unconventional,
humorous point of view of men

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:46.073
who sail new seas and blaze new
trails through the wilderness.'

00:03:46.073 --> 00:03:47.490
As they gathered
around the hearth

00:03:47.490 --> 00:03:50.860
one night after
dinner at Stormfield,

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:54.580
she records, 'Mr. Clemens stood
with his back to the fire,

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:56.630
talking to us.

00:03:56.630 --> 00:04:03.960
There he stood, our Mark Twain,
our American, our humorist,

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:07.190
the embodiment of our country.

00:04:07.190 --> 00:04:11.540
He seems to have absorbed
all America into himself.

00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:14.990
The great Mississippi River
seems forever flowing,

00:04:14.990 --> 00:04:18.310
flowing through his speech.'

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:21.430
When Twain took her to her
room to say good night,

00:04:21.430 --> 00:04:25.960
he said, I would find
cigars and a thermos bottle

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:28.960
with scotch whisky or
bourbon, if I preferred it,

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:31.480
in the bathroom.

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:33.220
One evening, Twain
offered to read to her

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:36.450
from his short
story, Eve's Diary.

00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:38.070
She was delighted.

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:41.070
And he asked, how
shall we arrange it?

00:04:41.070 --> 00:04:43.920
She said, oh, you
will read aloud,

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:48.570
and my teacher will spell
your words into my hand.

00:04:48.570 --> 00:04:52.800
He murmured, I had thought
you would read my lips.

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:55.280
So that's what she did.

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:59.640
Upon request, and as promised,
Twain put on his Oxford robe--

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:02.240
the gorgeous scarlet
robe he had worn

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:05.525
when Oxford University conferred
upon him the degree of Doctor

00:05:05.525 --> 00:05:08.120
of Letters.

00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:12.120
Here is Keller's
recollection of the evening.

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:15.150
'Mr. Clemens sat in
his great armchair,

00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:19.290
dressed in his white serge
suit, the flaming scarlet robe

00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:22.440
draping his shoulders, and
his white hair gleaming

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:25.920
and glistening in the light
of the lamp which shone down

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:27.690
on his head.

00:05:27.690 --> 00:05:33.080
In one hand, he held Eve's
Diary in a glorious red cover.

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:36.060
In the other hand,
he held his pipe.

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:38.400
I sat down near him
in a little chair,

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:42.090
my elbow on the arm of his
chair so that my fingers

00:05:42.090 --> 00:05:44.490
could rest lightly on his lips.

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:49.810
Everything went
smoothly for a time.

00:05:49.810 --> 00:05:53.800
But Twain's gesticulations
soon began to confuse things,

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:56.010
and so a new setting
was arranged.

00:05:56.010 --> 00:05:59.490
Mrs. Macy came and sat beside
me and spelled the words

00:05:59.490 --> 00:06:02.250
into my right hand
while I looked at Mr.

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:06.030
Clemens with my left,
touching his face and hands

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:09.660
and the book, following his
gestures and every changing

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:12.960
expression.'

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:16.500
Keller reflected that, 'to
one hampered and circumscribed

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:19.200
as I am, it was a
wonderful experience

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:22.120
to have a friend
like Mr. Clemens.

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:25.600
I recall many talks with
him about human affairs.

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:28.960
He never made me feel that
my opinions were worthless.

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:32.330
He knew that we do not think
with our ears and eyes,

00:06:32.330 --> 00:06:33.820
and that our
capacity for thought

00:06:33.820 --> 00:06:36.920
is not measured by five senses.

00:06:36.920 --> 00:06:39.680
He kept me always in
mind when we talked,

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:43.760
and he treated me like
a competent human being.

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:44.850
That is why I loved him.

00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:52.330
There was about him the air of
one who had suffered greatly.

00:06:52.330 --> 00:06:56.290
Whenever I touched his face,
his expression was sad,

00:06:56.290 --> 00:06:58.060
even when he was
telling a funny story.

00:06:58.060 --> 00:07:03.110
' He smiled not with his
mouth but with his mind--

00:07:03.110 --> 00:07:06.430
a gesture of the soul
rather than of the face.

00:07:06.430 --> 00:07:08.710
His voice was truly wonderful.

00:07:08.710 --> 00:07:12.850
To my touch, it
was deep, resonant.

00:07:12.850 --> 00:07:15.760
He had the power of modulating
it so as to suggest the most

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:17.730
delicate shades of meaning.

00:07:17.730 --> 00:07:19.780
And he spoke so
deliberately that I

00:07:19.780 --> 00:07:24.820
could get almost every word
with my fingers on his lips.

00:07:24.820 --> 00:07:27.370
Ah, how sweet and
poignant the memory

00:07:27.370 --> 00:07:32.320
of his soft, slow speech playing
over my listening fingers.

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:34.960
His words seemed to take
strange, lovely shapes

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:37.250
on my hands.

00:07:37.250 --> 00:07:40.497
His own hands were wonderfully
mobile and changeable

00:07:40.497 --> 00:07:41.830
under the influence of emotions.

00:07:45.450 --> 00:07:48.810
It has been said that life
has treated me harshly.

00:07:48.810 --> 00:07:51.720
And sometimes I have
complained in my heart,

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:53.790
because many pleasures
of human experience

00:07:53.790 --> 00:07:56.600
have been withheld from me.

00:07:56.600 --> 00:07:59.270
But when I recollect the
treasure of friendship

00:07:59.270 --> 00:08:02.930
that has been bestowed upon
me, I withdraw all charges

00:08:02.930 --> 00:08:05.090
against life.

00:08:05.090 --> 00:08:08.000
If much has been
denied me, much--

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:10.750
very much-- has been given me.

00:08:10.750 --> 00:08:13.480
So long as the memory of
certain beloved friends lives

00:08:13.480 --> 00:08:17.170
in my heart, I shall
say that life is good.'

00:08:17.170 --> 00:08:19.870
When Helen Keller left the
enchanted land of Stormfield

00:08:19.870 --> 00:08:23.020
on that visit, she wondered if
she would ever see her friend

00:08:23.020 --> 00:08:25.660
again, and she didn't.

00:08:25.660 --> 00:08:30.670
It was 1909, and Clemens
would live just one year more.

00:08:30.670 --> 00:08:34.299
But she writes for
us, 'In my fingertips

00:08:34.299 --> 00:08:37.059
was graven the image
of his dear face,

00:08:37.059 --> 00:08:39.940
with his halo of
shining white hair.

00:08:39.940 --> 00:08:44.650
And in my memory, his drawling,
marvelous voice will always

00:08:44.650 --> 00:08:47.750
vibrate.'"

00:08:47.750 --> 00:08:50.450
So that's Fingertip Memories.

00:08:50.450 --> 00:08:53.900
And here's another
one about an American

00:08:53.900 --> 00:08:56.980
whose name the
whole world knows--

00:08:56.980 --> 00:08:57.610
John Wayne.

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:05.470
"22-year-old Marion Morrison,
known to his friends as Duke,

00:09:05.470 --> 00:09:09.160
was carrying a table on his head
across the soundstage of a John

00:09:09.160 --> 00:09:10.960
Ford movie.

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:13.900
He was working as a
prop man in a Fox studio

00:09:13.900 --> 00:09:16.930
in LA early in 1930.

00:09:16.930 --> 00:09:20.110
Director Raoul Walsh was looking
for a leading man for an epic

00:09:20.110 --> 00:09:22.900
Western he was developing
about a great wagon

00:09:22.900 --> 00:09:26.200
train journeying across
vast deserts and mountains

00:09:26.200 --> 00:09:28.750
to California.

00:09:28.750 --> 00:09:31.960
Walsh didn't want a known
star to play the lead.

00:09:31.960 --> 00:09:34.360
He was looking for someone
who would, in his words,

00:09:34.360 --> 00:09:38.200
'be a true replica
of the pioneer type.'

00:09:38.200 --> 00:09:41.320
He didn't want the audience
to see a part being acted.

00:09:41.320 --> 00:09:43.570
He wanted them to
see the real thing.

00:09:43.570 --> 00:09:47.520
Again, in his words, 'someone to
get out there and act natural,

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:49.760
be himself.'

00:09:49.760 --> 00:09:52.360
Then he happened upon
the young Duke Morrison,

00:09:52.360 --> 00:09:56.290
lugging a table
across a soundstage."

00:09:56.290 --> 00:09:58.960
And here's Walsh recalling that.

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:03.280
"'He was in his early
20s and laughing.

00:10:03.280 --> 00:10:06.670
The expression on his face was
so warm and wholesome that I

00:10:06.670 --> 00:10:09.370
stopped and watched.

00:10:09.370 --> 00:10:13.390
I noticed the fine physique of
the boy, his careless strength,

00:10:13.390 --> 00:10:16.460
the grace of his movements.

00:10:16.460 --> 00:10:19.490
What I needed was a feeling
of honesty, of sincerity,

00:10:19.490 --> 00:10:22.300
and he had it.'

00:10:22.300 --> 00:10:25.390
Within a few weeks, after
a quick screen test,

00:10:25.390 --> 00:10:27.070
Duke would be signed
up for the part

00:10:27.070 --> 00:10:30.940
of Breck Coleman, the
fearless young scout,

00:10:30.940 --> 00:10:35.050
in an ambitious film to
be called The Big Trail.

00:10:35.050 --> 00:10:41.410
He would more than double his
income from $35 to $75 a week.

00:10:41.410 --> 00:10:45.680
He had to let his hair grow
long and learn to throw a knife.

00:10:45.680 --> 00:10:47.890
And he would have a new name--

00:10:47.890 --> 00:10:48.540
John Wayne.

00:10:51.530 --> 00:10:54.500
Already as the young
frontiersman in The Big Trail,

00:10:54.500 --> 00:10:56.000
the man the world
would come to know

00:10:56.000 --> 00:10:58.790
as John Wayne is recognizable.

00:10:58.790 --> 00:11:00.380
He is more athletic
and beautiful

00:11:00.380 --> 00:11:02.750
than we remember him
from his later pictures,

00:11:02.750 --> 00:11:04.820
and he has a sweetness
and shyness of youth

00:11:04.820 --> 00:11:07.040
that recedes over time.

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:10.110
But he is tough and in charge.

00:11:10.110 --> 00:11:13.100
He has a natural air of command.

00:11:13.100 --> 00:11:15.180
The widescreen film
is still visually

00:11:15.180 --> 00:11:17.260
stunning and
interesting to watch,

00:11:17.260 --> 00:11:21.030
but it was an epic flop and
left Wayne languishing in B

00:11:21.030 --> 00:11:23.940
movie purgatory
for almost a decade

00:11:23.940 --> 00:11:26.490
before John Ford
decided to make him

00:11:26.490 --> 00:11:33.220
a star as The Ringo Kid in
the great Western Stagecoach.

00:11:33.220 --> 00:11:36.070
Ford was inspired by something
similar to Raoul Walsh's

00:11:36.070 --> 00:11:40.165
inspiration when he chose Duke
Morrison for The Big Trail.

00:11:40.165 --> 00:11:42.540
And these are Ford's words.

00:11:42.540 --> 00:11:45.230
'It isn't enough for an
actor to look the part

00:11:45.230 --> 00:11:47.360
and say his lines well.

00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:48.860
Something else
has to come across

00:11:48.860 --> 00:11:51.710
to audiences, something
which no director can

00:11:51.710 --> 00:11:53.820
instill or create--

00:11:53.820 --> 00:11:57.360
the quality of
being a real man.'

00:11:57.360 --> 00:12:00.750
Ford added that Wayne 'was
the only person I could think

00:12:00.750 --> 00:12:03.780
of at the time who could
personify great strength

00:12:03.780 --> 00:12:08.380
and determination
without talking much.'

00:12:08.380 --> 00:12:11.110
That sounds easy,' says Ford.

00:12:11.110 --> 00:12:12.430
But it's not.

00:12:12.430 --> 00:12:15.730
Either you have
it or you don't.'

00:12:15.730 --> 00:12:17.890
John Wayne had it.

00:12:17.890 --> 00:12:20.440
As James Baldwin
wrote, 'One does not

00:12:20.440 --> 00:12:24.700
go to see Katharine Hepburn, or
Bette Davis, Humphrey Bogart,

00:12:24.700 --> 00:12:27.560
or John Wayne act.

00:12:27.560 --> 00:12:31.790
One goes to watch them be.'

00:12:31.790 --> 00:12:34.510
And Duke Morrison
decided that John Wayne

00:12:34.510 --> 00:12:37.300
would be the kind of
man he and the audience

00:12:37.300 --> 00:12:39.670
wanted to believe in.

00:12:39.670 --> 00:12:43.180
Whatever his flaws-- and
Wayne's characters had many--

00:12:43.180 --> 00:12:45.160
he would present on
screen a character that

00:12:45.160 --> 00:12:47.590
had something admirable in it.

00:12:47.590 --> 00:12:51.070
This character certainly added
dimensions with his great films

00:12:51.070 --> 00:12:54.140
like Red River, The Searchers.

00:12:54.140 --> 00:12:57.820
But his essence was discernible
from the earliest days.

00:12:57.820 --> 00:13:00.610
He had courage
and self-reliance,

00:13:00.610 --> 00:13:04.250
obstinacy, and even
ruthlessness, but also

00:13:04.250 --> 00:13:07.480
generosity of soul and spirit.

00:13:07.480 --> 00:13:09.880
As his biographer
Scott Eyman put it,

00:13:09.880 --> 00:13:12.340
'He had the kind of spirit
that makes fireman rush

00:13:12.340 --> 00:13:17.310
into a burning building because
it's the right thing to do.'

00:13:17.310 --> 00:13:20.930
He had humor,
gusto, irascibility.

00:13:20.930 --> 00:13:25.850
He was bold, defiant, ambitious,
heedless of consequences,

00:13:25.850 --> 00:13:31.880
occasionally mistaken, primarily
alone, larger than life.

00:13:31.880 --> 00:13:33.920
As one of Wayne's
colleagues said,

00:13:33.920 --> 00:13:37.280
'John Wayne was what every
young boy wants to be like,

00:13:37.280 --> 00:13:39.320
and what every old man
wishes he had been."

00:13:41.830 --> 00:13:46.030
Wayne was 32 when he made
Stagecoach and 69 when he made

00:13:46.030 --> 00:13:49.470
his last film, The Shootist.

00:13:49.470 --> 00:13:55.490
In that film, he plays the dying
gunfighter, John Bernard Books.

00:13:55.490 --> 00:13:57.650
His oft-quoted
line from that film

00:13:57.650 --> 00:14:01.250
would have been right at
home in The Big Trail.

00:14:01.250 --> 00:14:03.230
'I won't be wronged.

00:14:03.230 --> 00:14:05.260
I won't be insulted.

00:14:05.260 --> 00:14:07.450
I won't be laid a hand on.

00:14:07.450 --> 00:14:09.970
I don't do these
things to other people,

00:14:09.970 --> 00:14:13.630
and I require the
same from them.'

00:14:13.630 --> 00:14:18.470
For 25 years--
from 1949 to 1974--

00:14:18.470 --> 00:14:21.020
Wayne was among the
top 10 box office stars

00:14:21.020 --> 00:14:23.016
every year but one.

00:14:23.016 --> 00:14:26.060
And he was more than
a star for his time.

00:14:26.060 --> 00:14:30.530
Well into the 21st century,
35 years after his death,

00:14:30.530 --> 00:14:33.230
he was still listed as one of
America's five favorite movie

00:14:33.230 --> 00:14:34.670
stars.

00:14:34.670 --> 00:14:37.730
He became 'indivisibly
associated with America

00:14:37.730 --> 00:14:41.020
itself,' in Eyman's words.

00:14:41.020 --> 00:14:45.520
On his 72nd birthday,
May 26, 1979,

00:14:45.520 --> 00:14:49.900
as Wayne lay dying of cancer at
UCLA Medical Center, the United

00:14:49.900 --> 00:14:53.860
States Congress, in a
unanimous bipartisan vote,

00:14:53.860 --> 00:14:56.970
approved an order signed
by President Jimmy Carter

00:14:56.970 --> 00:15:00.860
for striking a congressional
gold medal in his honor.

00:15:00.860 --> 00:15:04.480
Wayne would be the 85th
recipient of the medal.

00:15:04.480 --> 00:15:07.910
The first recipient
was George Washington.

00:15:07.910 --> 00:15:11.060
Winston Churchill was awarded
the medal just a few years

00:15:11.060 --> 00:15:13.670
before John Wayne.

00:15:13.670 --> 00:15:17.210
As President Carter said,
'Wayne's ruggedness,

00:15:17.210 --> 00:15:20.270
the tough independence, the
sense of personal conviction

00:15:20.270 --> 00:15:23.720
and courage on and off the
screen reflected the best

00:15:23.720 --> 00:15:26.240
of our national character.'

00:15:26.240 --> 00:15:28.490
Wayne's friend,
actress Maureen O'Hara,

00:15:28.490 --> 00:15:32.480
testifying before
Congress, said,

00:15:32.480 --> 00:15:35.090
'To the people of
the world, John Wayne

00:15:35.090 --> 00:15:38.920
is not just an actor
and a very fine actor.

00:15:38.920 --> 00:15:42.940
John Wayne is the United
States of America.

00:15:42.940 --> 00:15:45.330
He is what they
believe it to be.

00:15:45.330 --> 00:15:47.970
He is what they hope it will be.

00:15:47.970 --> 00:15:52.048
And he is what they hope
it will always be.'"

00:15:52.048 --> 00:15:55.060
So that's a John Wayne story.

00:15:55.060 --> 00:15:57.020
And here's one about
an American whose name

00:15:57.020 --> 00:15:58.820
won't be as familiar
to you, probably.

00:15:58.820 --> 00:16:01.880
But you will want
to know it, I hope.

00:16:01.880 --> 00:16:06.620
It's called, We're
All Americans.

00:16:06.620 --> 00:16:10.490
"Eli Parker was born in 1828
to Elizabeth and William

00:16:10.490 --> 00:16:14.330
Parker of the Tonawanda
Seneca tribe of the Iroquois

00:16:14.330 --> 00:16:17.410
Confederacy in Western New York.

00:16:17.410 --> 00:16:20.590
Parker became a leader in his
tribe at a very young age,

00:16:20.590 --> 00:16:23.200
trained as a civil
engineer, and earned himself

00:16:23.200 --> 00:16:25.690
a reputation in that field.

00:16:25.690 --> 00:16:29.170
In 1857, when he
was 29 years old,

00:16:29.170 --> 00:16:32.530
he moved to Galena, Illinois
as a civil engineer,

00:16:32.530 --> 00:16:34.730
working for the
Treasury Department.

00:16:34.730 --> 00:16:38.240
And there his life
took a fateful turn.

00:16:38.240 --> 00:16:42.670
He became friends with a
fellow named Ulysses S. Grant.

00:16:42.670 --> 00:16:45.520
In these years, Grant
was an ex-army officer,

00:16:45.520 --> 00:16:48.920
working as a clerk in
his father's store.

00:16:48.920 --> 00:16:52.490
Parker later liked to tell the
story of coming to Grant's aid

00:16:52.490 --> 00:16:54.980
in a bar room fight in Galena--

00:16:54.980 --> 00:16:57.950
the two of them back to
back, fighting their way out

00:16:57.950 --> 00:17:01.440
against practically
all the other patrons.

00:17:01.440 --> 00:17:04.800
At about 5 feet 8
inches and 200 pounds,

00:17:04.800 --> 00:17:09.240
the robust Parker referred
to himself as a 'savage Jack

00:17:09.240 --> 00:17:12.290
Falstaff.'

00:17:12.290 --> 00:17:16.010
When the Civil War came
on, Parker several times

00:17:16.010 --> 00:17:18.930
tried to join the Union
Army as an engineer

00:17:18.930 --> 00:17:22.160
but was turned down because
he was not a citizen.

00:17:22.160 --> 00:17:24.140
When he approached
Secretary of State William

00:17:24.140 --> 00:17:26.240
Seward about a
commission, he was

00:17:26.240 --> 00:17:29.870
told the war was an
affair between white men,

00:17:29.870 --> 00:17:31.010
that he should go home.

00:17:31.010 --> 00:17:33.410
We will settle our own
troubles among ourselves

00:17:33.410 --> 00:17:38.150
without any Indian aid,
thank you very much.

00:17:38.150 --> 00:17:40.400
Eventually, with
Grant's endorsement,

00:17:40.400 --> 00:17:43.190
Parker received a commission
with the rank of Captain

00:17:43.190 --> 00:17:46.340
as assistant adjutant
general for volunteers.

00:17:46.340 --> 00:17:50.180
By late 1863, he had been
transferred to Grant's staff

00:17:50.180 --> 00:17:52.430
as military secretary.

00:17:52.430 --> 00:17:56.510
He soon became familiarly known
as the Indian at headquarters

00:17:56.510 --> 00:17:58.220
and was promoted to
lieutenant colonel

00:17:58.220 --> 00:18:01.160
and later to brigadier general.

00:18:01.160 --> 00:18:03.680
He may have saved Grant's
life, or at least prevented

00:18:03.680 --> 00:18:06.080
his capture, on a night
in the wilderness campaign

00:18:06.080 --> 00:18:09.110
in 1864, when
Grant and his staff

00:18:09.110 --> 00:18:11.660
were riding into enemy
lines in the dark,

00:18:11.660 --> 00:18:13.940
unbeknownst to themselves.

00:18:13.940 --> 00:18:16.282
But Parker is rightly
most remembered

00:18:16.282 --> 00:18:17.990
for something that
happened in the parlor

00:18:17.990 --> 00:18:20.960
of a private residence in
the village of Appomattox

00:18:20.960 --> 00:18:26.380
Courthouse on April 9, 1865.

00:18:26.380 --> 00:18:28.780
In the days preceding,
Union armies

00:18:28.780 --> 00:18:31.060
had captured the
city of Petersburg

00:18:31.060 --> 00:18:33.550
and the Confederate
capital of Richmond.

00:18:33.550 --> 00:18:36.340
Grant and the federal
army at the Potomac

00:18:36.340 --> 00:18:38.200
had put Confederate
General Robert E.

00:18:38.200 --> 00:18:42.100
Lee and the Army of Northern
Virginia in such a position

00:18:42.100 --> 00:18:45.070
that in the late
afternoon of April 7,

00:18:45.070 --> 00:18:48.220
Grant, sitting on the veranda
of his hotel headquarters

00:18:48.220 --> 00:18:52.580
in Farmville, said to a
couple of his generals,

00:18:52.580 --> 00:18:57.560
I have a great mind to
summon Lee, to surrender.

00:18:57.560 --> 00:18:59.930
He immediately wrote a
letter, respectfully inviting

00:18:59.930 --> 00:19:02.060
Lee to surrender, and
had it sent to him

00:19:02.060 --> 00:19:04.930
under a flag of truce.

00:19:04.930 --> 00:19:08.260
It took Lee a couple of days
of desperate failed maneuvers

00:19:08.260 --> 00:19:10.430
to come around to the idea.

00:19:10.430 --> 00:19:13.810
But by the morning of April
9, Lee had concluded--

00:19:13.810 --> 00:19:15.640
and these are his words--

00:19:15.640 --> 00:19:19.690
that 'there is nothing left me
to do but to go and see General

00:19:19.690 --> 00:19:24.910
Grant, and I would
rather die 1,000 deaths.'

00:19:24.910 --> 00:19:26.950
They agreed to meet in
the village of Appomattox

00:19:26.950 --> 00:19:30.510
Courthouse to discuss terms.

00:19:30.510 --> 00:19:32.580
Grant had been
riding hard for days

00:19:32.580 --> 00:19:35.040
on rough roads in rough weather.

00:19:35.040 --> 00:19:37.260
When he met Lee in the
parlor of the brick house

00:19:37.260 --> 00:19:39.150
where they had
arranged to meet, he

00:19:39.150 --> 00:19:43.250
had on dirty boots, an old
suit without his sword,

00:19:43.250 --> 00:19:46.290
and without any
distinguishing mark of rank,

00:19:46.290 --> 00:19:48.510
except the shoulder
straps of the lieutenant

00:19:48.510 --> 00:19:51.830
general on a woolen blouse.

00:19:51.830 --> 00:19:56.090
Lee was decked out from head to
toe in all the military finery

00:19:56.090 --> 00:19:59.010
he had at his disposal.

00:19:59.010 --> 00:20:02.070
After introductions and
not much small talk,

00:20:02.070 --> 00:20:04.320
Lee asked Grant on what
terms he would receive

00:20:04.320 --> 00:20:06.810
the surrender of Lee's army.

00:20:06.810 --> 00:20:09.570
Grant told him, all
officers and men

00:20:09.570 --> 00:20:11.910
would be paroled
and disqualified

00:20:11.910 --> 00:20:15.450
from taking up arms again
until properly exchanged.

00:20:15.450 --> 00:20:18.090
And all arms
ammunition and supplies

00:20:18.090 --> 00:20:21.960
were to be delivered up
as captured property.

00:20:21.960 --> 00:20:24.390
Lee said those were
the terms he expected,

00:20:24.390 --> 00:20:25.800
and he asked Grant
to commit them

00:20:25.800 --> 00:20:31.080
to writing, which he did on the
spot, and showed them to Lee.

00:20:31.080 --> 00:20:33.900
With minor revisions,
Lee accepted,

00:20:33.900 --> 00:20:36.420
and Grant handed the document
to his senior adjutant

00:20:36.420 --> 00:20:40.570
general, Theodore
Bowers, to put into ink.

00:20:40.570 --> 00:20:43.820
In Grant's words,
'this was a document

00:20:43.820 --> 00:20:45.950
that would effectively
put an end to four

00:20:45.950 --> 00:20:49.060
years of devastating Civil War.

00:20:49.060 --> 00:20:53.260
Bowers' hands were so unsteady
from nerves that he had

00:20:53.260 --> 00:20:55.900
to start over three
or four times,

00:20:55.900 --> 00:20:58.990
going through several sheets
of paper in a failed effort

00:20:58.990 --> 00:21:03.160
to prepare a fair copy for the
signatures of the generals.'

00:21:03.160 --> 00:21:06.370
So Grant asked Eli
Parker to do it,

00:21:06.370 --> 00:21:09.770
which he did without trouble.

00:21:09.770 --> 00:21:13.980
This gave occasion for Lee
and Parker to be introduced.

00:21:13.980 --> 00:21:17.190
When Lee recognized that
Parker was an American Indian,

00:21:17.190 --> 00:21:23.000
he said, 'I'm glad to see
one real American here.'

00:21:23.000 --> 00:21:25.940
Parker shook his
hand and replied,

00:21:25.940 --> 00:21:30.520
'We are all Americans.'"

00:21:30.520 --> 00:21:32.560
The American story
is still young

00:21:32.560 --> 00:21:35.800
and is already the
greatest story ever written

00:21:35.800 --> 00:21:38.540
by human hands and minds.

00:21:38.540 --> 00:21:41.050
It is a story of freedom
the likes of which

00:21:41.050 --> 00:21:43.370
the world had never seen.

00:21:43.370 --> 00:21:47.060
It is endlessly
interesting and instructive

00:21:47.060 --> 00:21:49.280
and will continue
unfolding in word and deed

00:21:49.280 --> 00:21:52.440
as long as there are Americans.

00:21:52.440 --> 00:21:54.560
The stories I think
are most important

00:21:54.560 --> 00:21:58.340
are those about what it is that
makes America beautiful, what

00:21:58.340 --> 00:22:03.760
it is that makes America good,
and therefore, worthy of love.

00:22:03.760 --> 00:22:05.890
Only in this light,
I think, can we

00:22:05.890 --> 00:22:08.800
see clearly what it is
that might make America

00:22:08.800 --> 00:22:12.010
better and more beautiful.

00:22:12.010 --> 00:22:13.860
Thank you.

